// Practice quiz: Arrays and objects: map, filter, reduce
// First draft: unreviewed. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/javascript-fundamentals/arrays-and-objects-map-filter-reduce

::o1 missing property::const user \= \{ name\: "Ada" \}; What is user.age? {
=undefined #Correct. Reading a missing property gives undefined rather than an error.
~null #Not quite. JavaScript does not supply null automatically for a missing property.
~0 #Not quite. There is no numeric default.
~A ReferenceError is thrown #Not quite. That error is for undeclared variables, not missing properties.
}

::o1 key in a variable::const key \= "price"; How do you read that property of item? {
=item[key] #Correct. Brackets evaluate the variable and use its value as the key.
~item.key #Not quite. That reads a property literally named key.
~item.(key) #Not quite. That is not valid syntax.
~item\:\:key #Not quite. JavaScript has no such member operator.
}

::o2 forgetting the return::A filter callback has a block body that tests n, greater than 10, but has no return statement. What does filter produce? {
=An empty array, because the callback returns undefined each time #Correct. undefined is falsy, so no item is kept.
~The original array #Not quite. Items are kept only for truthy results.
~A TypeError #Not quite. A callback that returns nothing is not an error.
~Only the numbers above 10 #Not quite. That needs the condition to be returned.
}

::o2 which method::You need one new string for each order in an array. Which method fits? {
=map #Correct. map produces one new value per item.
~filter #Not quite. filter chooses items; it does not transform them.
~forEach #Not quite. forEach returns undefined, so you get no new array.
~reduce with no initial value #Not quite. That folds to one value and breaks on empty input.
}

::o3 reduce on empty::What does [].reduce with an adding callback and no initial value do? {
=It throws a TypeError #Correct. With no items and no initial value there is nothing to start from.
~It returns 0 #Not quite. That needs an explicit initial value of 0.
~It returns undefined #Not quite. It throws rather than returning.
~It returns an empty array #Not quite. reduce returns the folded value; there is none here.
}
